--- body ---
## Context

`buildIncremental` calls `reset()`: despite the name, every build is a full
rebuild, and `builtFromVersion` holds an item count rather than a library
version. So an added or deleted document is invisible until someone rebuilds by
hand, a deleted one keeps answering queries, and `zotero_semantic_search`'s
auto-build only fires on an *empty* index — a stale one never self-heals.
Tracker: 0001.

Verified on the Zotero 10 local API:

    Last-Modified-Version: 200          one header, one request
    ?since=195&limit=100  -> 3 of 7540  a real delta
    ?format=versions      -> key -> version map for the whole library
    /deleted?since=0      -> 404        cloud-only; deletions need a key-set reconcile

## Design

`deleteByItem` is already on 0002's `PassageStore` port, and the indexed
`passage_meta_item` index is why it is affordable -- 0 ms against 362 ms for
the `UNINDEXED`-column shape 0002 rejected. That measurement was taken for this
ticket: a fifty-item delta on the scanning shape would cost eighteen seconds.

**The watermark lives in the database, with its provenance.** A `meta` table of
key/value, holding `builtFromVersion` and `backend` (`'local' | 'web'`). Local
and cloud version sequences are unrelated integers, so a watermark without its
backend label is worse than none: switching a user from the desktop API to the
Web API would compare two incommensurable numbers and either skip a real delta
or replay the library. On a backend mismatch, ignore the watermark and rebuild.

**Deletions need a key-set reconcile.** `/deleted?since=` is cloud-only and
404s on the Zotero 10 local API (verified). So deletions come from
`?format=versions`, which returns a key -> version map for the whole library:
diff its key set against `SELECT DISTINCT item FROM passage_meta` and delete
the difference. That is one request and one indexed query, not a scan.

**Freshness at query time, not a background poll.** One request for
`Last-Modified-Version`. Equal to the watermark, serve; greater, apply the
delta first. This is also what fixes the stale-index hole in the current
design: `zotero_semantic_search`'s auto-build only fires on an *empty* index,
so a stale one never self-heals today.

**Attachments not yet extracted are recorded, not skipped.** A row with the
item present and no text, so a later delta picks it up. Skipping makes the
absence indistinguishable from an item that has no attachment, and the delta
never revisits it.

## Actions

1. `meta` table with `builtFromVersion` and `backend`; mismatch forces rebuild.
2. Freshness check at query time: one `Last-Modified-Version` request.
3. Additions and modifications via `?since=`; `deleteByItem` for what changed,
   then re-insert.
4. Deletions by reconciling `?format=versions` against `SELECT DISTINCT item`.
5. Attachments awaiting extraction recorded as present-without-text;
   `fullTextSince` already exists on both clients.

## Test

Red step: build an index over three items, delete one from the fixture library,
run the delta, and assert a query that matched it returns nothing. This is the
one behaviour the current design cannot express at all -- `buildIncremental`
calls `reset()`, so today the only way to forget a document is to rebuild.

## Invariants

- A deleted item must stop returning hits. This is the one behaviour the
  current design cannot express at all.

## Findings from the implementation

**The red step failed first, correctly.** Against pre-change code the deletion
test reported `expected [ 'BBB' ] to deeply equal []` -- build, routing and
query all worked and the removed item kept answering, which is exactly the
behaviour the old design cannot express.

**The freshness check is bounded five ways**, none of which can turn a search
into a failure: `supportsDelta` is false on the JSON backend (zero requests,
zero change); it stands down while `isBuilding`; a per-context single-flight
promise makes two concurrent queries share one probe; an 8 s wall-clock
deadline is checked before each request and raced against the first -- racing
bounds the *wait*, not the request, since nothing here can cancel a fetch in
flight; a change set over 500 items (50 with full text) becomes a background
rebuild rather than an inline crawl; and any throw degrades to serving the
current index, with a 60 s backoff so a closed desktop app does not cost a
connection failure per query. The watermark advances only after the whole delta
lands, so an abandoned refresh is retried rather than skipped.

**`createFulltextSource` contained the very skip this ticket forbids.** When
`fullTextSince(0)` returned empty it took an early return and built an inert
source without walking attachments at all -- precisely the case where *every*
attachment is pending. That is what made the step-5 test fail on its first run.
It now walks on a short leash (20 pages, 2 000-item cap) purely to record them.

**`build()`, the non-incremental path, had to be made to clear the label.** It
knows only a list of items, so recording a version under the previous build's
label would manufacture exactly the incomparable-pair-that-looks-comparable the
label exists to prevent.

**A restart with zero extracted attachments had no evidence the index wanted
full text** -- no full-text passages means `fulltextEnabled` reads false, and a
metadata-only delta would then have wiped the pending record permanently. A
non-empty pending set now counts as that evidence.

**A cancelled build records no watermark; a cap-truncated one does.**
Truncation is stable and part of what the build set out to cover;
`requestStop()` is an arbitrary point above which nothing was read.

**Deltas can drift the index above `indexMaxItems`** over time, since `?since=`
does not respect the crawl's page order. Left unclamped deliberately -- refusing
to index something the user just edited would be worse -- with a comment saying
so.

**Still not restored after a restart:** `itemsFetched` / `itemsTotal` /
`itemsAvailable`, which are build-progress telemetry and were never persisted.
One consequence worth knowing: `truncationNotice()` keys on
`itemsAvailable > itemsTotal`, so after a restart a cap-truncated index stops
warning that part of the library is unsearchable. Pre-existing, not widened
here, left alone.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Add a document, query, find it, without a manual rebuild
- [x] Delete a document, query, do not find it
- [ ] Add an attachment and open it in Zotero, then find its text -- BLOCKED on
      a real Zotero pass. Fixture coverage exists and is not the same thing
      (review 2026-08-21)
- [x] Watermark check costs one local request -- pinned by a call-count test
